|~ `::keyword` and what is declared under it: its constructor and its
   refusal, the comparisons of keywords, and the words that turn a
   keyword into a string, a number and a tag.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114]. ~|

:keyword
  |~~ A keyword is a name that stands for itself, `:name`. It is apart
      from the string so that no name is read by its look: every key of
      a map is a keyword, `/k` reads the field a keyword names, and a
      keyword names a declaration, `:x 5` declaring `x`.

      A bare keyword is a colon and a name that begins with a letter of
      any script, `_` or `@` and goes on with letters, digits, `_` and
      `-`, `:id`, `:first-name`; a slash joins names into a path,
      `:domain/user`; any other text is quoted, `:"first name"`, and
      quotes around a bare name change nothing. A keyword is no string,
      `:a | eq "a"` answering `false`, and `toKeyword` and `toString`
      turn one into the other, `"draft" | toKeyword` answering `:draft`. JSON writes a
      keyword as its name, `"draft"`, and keywords rank after strings
      and before tags.
      ::snippet~(::keyword | manifest) lists its words.
      ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::string | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:keyword/isAKeyword
  |~~ `:k` is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:k | type | eq ::keyword)

:keyword/buildsAKeyword
  |~~ The constructor of a keyword answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::keyword:k | eq :k)

:keyword/takesThreeForms
  |~~ A name of any script, a name with a path and a quoted name are keywords alike. ~~|
  ::law~([:данные :domain/user :"$ref"] | map ~toString | eq ["данные" "domain/user" "$ref"])

:keyword/quotesABareName
  |~~ A bare name quoted is the same keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:"foo" | eq :foo)

:keyword/construct
  |~~ Reads a keyword as itself. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/PayloadNotKeywordError
  |~~ `::keyword` reads its payload as the keyword it is; a string
      becomes one by `toKeyword`.
  ~~|

:keyword/PayloadNotKeywordError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string payload is refused; `toKeyword` turns a string into one. ~~|
  ::law~(::keyword"k" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::keyword/PayloadNotKeywordError ::string])

:keyword/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes after the keyword the
      call gives, `:b | gt :a`. Keywords compare by their names as they
      are spelled, letter by letter, so a capital comes before a small
      letter, `:a | gt :B` answering `true`. A keyword compares with a
      keyword alone: a string given is refused under the value, so text
      read from JSON, which arrives as a string, is turned by `toKeyword`
      first, `"b" | toKeyword | gt :a`, or compared with a string. `gte`
      answers whether the subject comes after the value or is the same
      keyword, `lt` and `lte` the reverse.
      ::link~(::keyword/gte | doc) ::link~(::keyword/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::keyword/lte | doc) ::link~(::string/toKeyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/gt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/gt/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | gt :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/gt/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | gt :a`; a string is
      turned by `toKeyword` first, `:b | gt ("a" | toKeyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/gt/ordersByName
  |~~ `:b` comes after `:a`. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gt :a)

:keyword/gt/putsACapitalFirst
  |~~ A capital comes before a small letter. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | gt :B)

:keyword/gt/keepsOneKeywordApartFromItself
  |~~ A keyword does not come after itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | gt :a | not)

:keyword/gt/comparesTextTurnedIntoAKeyword
  |~~ Text turned by `toKeyword` compares with a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | toKeyword | gt :a)

:keyword/gt/value/NotKeywordError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | gt "a" !| type | eq ::keyword/gt/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes after the keyword the
      call gives or is the same keyword, `:b | gte :b`. It compares as
      `gt` does, keywords with keywords.
      ::link~(::keyword/gt | doc) ::link~(::keyword/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/gte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or is the same
      keyword, `false` otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/gte/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | gte :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/gte/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | gte :a`; a string is
      turned by `toKeyword` first, `:b | gte ("a" | toKeyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A keyword comes after or is itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gte :b)

:keyword/gte/ordersByName
  |~~ `:b` comes after `:a`. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gte :a)

:keyword/gte/value/NotKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gte 1 !| type | eq ::keyword/gte/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes before the keyword the
      call gives, `:a | lt :b`. It compares as `gt` does, keywords with
      keywords, a capital before a small letter, `:B | lt :a`.
      ::link~(::keyword/gt | doc) ::link~(::keyword/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/lt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/lt/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | lt :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/lt/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | lt :a`; a string is
      turned by `toKeyword` first, `:b | lt ("a" | toKeyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/lt/ordersByName
  |~~ `:a` comes before `:b`. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lt :b)

:keyword/lt/putsACapitalFirst
  |~~ A capital comes before a small letter. ~~|
  ::law~(:B | lt :a)

:keyword/lt/keepsOneKeywordApartFromItself
  |~~ A keyword does not come before itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lt :a | not)

:keyword/lt/value/NotKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | lt 1 !| type | eq ::keyword/lt/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes before the keyword the
      call gives or is the same keyword, `:a | lte :a`. It compares as
      `gt` does, keywords with keywords.
      ::link~(::keyword/lt | doc) ::link~(::keyword/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/lte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or is the same
      keyword, `false` otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/lte/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | lte :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/lte/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | lte :a`; a string is
      turned by `toKeyword` first, `:b | lte ("a" | toKeyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A keyword comes before or is itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lte :a)

:keyword/lte/ordersByName
  |~~ `:a` comes before `:b`. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lte :b)

:keyword/lte/value/NotKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | lte 1 !| type | eq ::keyword/lte/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/toString
  |~~ Answers the name of the keyword as a string, `:foo | toString`
      answering `"foo"`, the text JSON writes for it, so a key joins text,
      `{:a "x"} | entries | map ~([(/0 | toString) "=" /1] | join "")`. A
      quoted name and a name with a path answer their text as written,
      `:"foo bar"` the string `"foo bar"`. `toKeyword` turns the string
      back into the keyword.
      ::link~(::string/toKeyword | doc) ::link~(::keyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/toString/answer
  |~~ The name of the keyword, a string. ~~|
  ::string

:keyword/toString/answersTheName
  |~~ A keyword answers its name as written. ~~|
  ::law~([:foo :"foo bar" :domain/user] | map ~toString | eq ["foo" "foo bar" "domain/user"])

:keyword/toString/readsBack
  |~~ A keyword turned into its name turns back into itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:baz | toString | toKeyword | eq :baz)

:keyword/toNumber
  |~~ Answers the number the name of the keyword writes,
      `:"1" | toNumber` answering `1`, so a key that `groupBy` filed a
      number under turns back into the number,
      `groupBy ~(/year) | keys | toVec | map ~toNumber`. A name that
      writes no number is refused.
      ::link~(::number/toKeyword | doc) ::link~(::string/toNumber | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/toNumber/answer
  |~~ The number the name writes. ~~|
  ::number

:keyword/toNumber/NameNotNumberError
  |~~ `toNumber` reads the name of the keyword as the literal of a
      number, as a key that `groupBy` filed a number under writes it,
      `:"1"`; a name such as `:a` writes none.
  ~~|

:keyword/toNumber/readsTheNumberOfAKey
  |~~ A key filed from a number turns back into it. ~~|
  ::law~([{:y 2020} {:y 2021}] | groupBy ~(/y) | keys | toVec | map ~toNumber | eq [2020 2021])

:keyword/toNumber/NameNotNumberError/refusesAName
  |~~ A name that writes no number is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | toNumber !| type | eq ::keyword/toNumber/NameNotNumberError)

:keyword/toTag
  |~~ Answers the tag the keyword names, `:Box | toTag` answering
      `::Box`; a quoted name that names no tag is refused. `toKeyword`
      turns the tag back into the keyword.
      ::link~(::string/toTag | doc) ::link~(::tag/toKeyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/toTag/answer
  |~~ The tag the keyword names. ~~|
  ::tag

:keyword/toTag/NameNotNameError
  |~~ `toTag` reads the name of the keyword as the name of a tag; a
      quoted name with a space, `:"a b"`, names none.
  ~~|

:keyword/toTag/namesATag
  |~~ A keyword names the tag of its name. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box | toTag | eq ::Box)

:keyword/toTag/NameNotNameError/refusesASpace
  |~~ A name with a space names no tag. ~~|
  ::law~(:"a b" | toTag !| type | eq ::keyword/toTag/NameNotNameError)

:keyword/toKeyword
  |~~ Answers the keyword itself, so `map ~toKeyword` turns names,
      strings and numbers into keys, `["a" :b 1] | map ~toKeyword`
      answering `[:a :b :"1"]`.
      ::link~(::string/toKeyword | doc) ::link~(::number/toKeyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/toKeyword/answer
  |~~ The keyword itself. ~~|
  ::keyword

:keyword/toKeyword/answersItself
  |~~ A keyword answers itself, so names, strings and numbers turn into keys. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" :b 1] | map ~toKeyword | eq [:a :b :"1"])
